   "created_utc": "2020-07-20T20:54:41Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
   "body_html": "You can have valgrind log all allocations, see <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/30512000/how-to-make-valgrind-log-all-allocations\" title=\"how to make valgrind log all allocations\">stackoverflow.com/questions/30512000/&hellip;</a>.  Then when you manage to trigger the large consumption, you&#39;ll be able to see what part of your code is to blame."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 111414531,
   "parent_id": 63003465,
   "parent_type": "question",
   "author_user_id": 4641116,
   "author_display_name": "Eljay",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/63003465/#comment111414531_63003465",
   "score": 2,
   "created_utc": "2020-07-20T21:05:23Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
   "body_html": "Leaks that disappear at exit probably are not leaks, but rather memory resources that are held on too long (longer than needed).  One mechanism I&#39;ve used is have classes register with a memory monitor singleton class (I&#39;d use a string literal with the name of the class and have that address be the key), and the string which keeps track of total number of objects allocated, current number of live objects, and highwater mark of live objects.  Periodically (say once an hour for a program that runs for days), dump that information out to your log."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 111415534,
   "parent_id": 63003465,
   "parent_type": "question",
   "author_user_id": 128940,
   "author_display_name": "Beta",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/63003465/#comment111415534_63003465",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2020-07-20T21:53:47Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
   "body_html": "How do you <i>expect</i> memory usage to grow? Does that blue line represent memory usage? It seems to jump up and then plateau for a while, which suggests that a container may have filled up, and been enlarged. If you monitor 9 cameras instead of 10, does the plateau change? Does it rise less high, or appear later?"
  },
